rac{7}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{7}{2},0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{7}{2},0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{2}]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115858/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115858"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection/4fefbb6ba112097d0c04ef22a7fedff119cbda244436f88bcaa6a51fbe07ea8f" :answer/id #uuid "f139b0e3-6c87-500b-a855-ed54af59c65d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection/905df4df60cbeedb3e2943cabbda767728b15131ac33fc8d800102cc7e249931" :answer/id #uuid "7cb9d2f5-e58a-593c-9872-ead2c93ba613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection/f4b2153dd620072cad8c8a9b97a5a1e00b91e9b0d4a083e19156b3681d9eb1c9" :answer/id #uuid "a178568b-abd5-5e5f-9d8a-8cb8619ea5af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection/dd19760dbaf4d69d7e49040ed0ff5c8b79491ba02ee6a85ffe20c885a1e9c708" :answer/id #uuid "9ac52963-46f3-5cc8-9977-0f8edbdae8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection/6051697745b6f4b581c6a68ae3a678f9a839bf57245df240237bcf10950bf97f" :answer/id #uuid "591c34cf-802e-5ad5-b249-93ac58b23f6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e399fe-c904-5769-9c9b-6452ee920671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115832/selection/4fefbb6ba112097d0c04ef22a7fedff119cbda244436f88bcaa6a51fbe07ea8f" :answer-field/choices ["q-115832/selection/4fefbb6ba112097d0c04ef22a7fedff119cbda244436f88bcaa6a51fbe07ea8f" "q-115832/selection/905df4df60cbeedb3e2943cabbda767728b15131ac33fc8d800102cc7e249931" "q-115832/selection/f4b2153dd620072cad8c8a9b97a5a1e00b91e9b0d4a083e19156b3681d9eb1c9" "q-115832/selection/dd19760dbaf4d69d7e49040ed0ff5c8b79491ba02ee6a85ffe20c885a1e9c708" "q-115832/selection/6051697745b6f4b581c6a68ae3a678f9a839bf57245df240237bcf10950bf97f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115832" :question/id #uuid "269a576b-c401-5abc-a7cf-817b5e5f8580" :question/math-academy-id "q-115832" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{(x+4)(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptotes at $x=-4$ and $x=3.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-4,3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-4,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115832/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115832"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c56391d2-cd5b-53be-a63d-5cf30edcd35c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "be0f0714-41dc-5cb0-a351-3ae7ae1ef254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "009672d7-aabe-5f10-8b88-418ee309de54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326046/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-326046/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-326046/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "6017ce45-4fee-51ce-8172-61d99294c54a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca3211e6-3995-5e98-80ad-5d188a9540e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326046/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-326046/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326046" :question/id #uuid "b4b10d23-31c8-5e8e-b6dd-43ac8cb510ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-326046" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $h(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $h(x)=0$ has no solution because ${x}^{2}+1>0$ for all real $x.$ On the other hand, $h(x)$ has a single vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ This asymptote divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $h(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $h(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $h(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $h(x)$ is positive on the interval $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nh(x)>0\\text{ for all }x\\in (\\,3,\\infty \\,).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-326046/field-1" "q-326046/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-326046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection/f4d0da7238fde646b8614062a613eeecf144ee1754e5241cc019c52af93f828a" :answer/id #uuid "bce7993c-da0e-5e7e-b722-5103c1342d7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection/dde4b9ca05f22a3455a6c5e92d90bbf2d8ee93f33340ea1ba6a07931a1a97303" :answer/id #uuid "fe3a891f-8e33-5154-b617-7a12edf4e9d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection/ef500f71a37d8a98f4a4a10d2960847cb7099cb5ab04cb72b222d9616dc20a81" :answer/id #uuid "dcea36f7-9a97-522e-a9d5-56871f651f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection/eefdcb514f1c7bcaee981ab25056b9a5de51178c5e1b68f59cc80588be1821fc" :answer/id #uuid "b45afb06-5628-5251-bf68-576c9be9b17a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection/0cc500ddc40b712a99ae37e1ad667cd634ada8666aa1aaf0903b5daeb120f488" :answer/id #uuid "31c3301e-9546-5cf4-bf5e-5e37244bf670" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "801cf3ad-e340-5c9c-9c52-0ff0d72f297d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-88712/selection/dde4b9ca05f22a3455a6c5e92d90bbf2d8ee93f33340ea1ba6a07931a1a97303" :answer-field/choices ["q-88712/selection/f4d0da7238fde646b8614062a613eeecf144ee1754e5241cc019c52af93f828a" "q-88712/selection/dde4b9ca05f22a3455a6c5e92d90bbf2d8ee93f33340ea1ba6a07931a1a97303" "q-88712/selection/ef500f71a37d8a98f4a4a10d2960847cb7099cb5ab04cb72b222d9616dc20a81" "q-88712/selection/eefdcb514f1c7bcaee981ab25056b9a5de51178c5e1b68f59cc80588be1821fc" "q-88712/selection/0cc500ddc40b712a99ae37e1ad667cd634ada8666aa1aaf0903b5daeb120f488"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88712" :question/id #uuid "60ff8676-b37f-57cf-bd9e-eed47914a514" :question/math-academy-id "q-88712" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(2x-1)(x-2)}{x+2}\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-1)(x-2)}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},2].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-88712/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dedebdf-6f3d-5982-a29a-9985de7f949f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88712"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection/144cf194032fb6a518bc4749829b072ca42940329a3c89593e06f6b9dbf7895a" :answer/id #uuid "3b86bdb5-bbce-54d6-9221-ece08dc61e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection/6cd7ba537c06f069990dfd5ed134e4f73bdea283b926cec942149fb50cdcfb3b" :answer/id #uuid "0e39d66f-784b-553d-aa86-332fd98fc1ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection/b50005a2c8c4787e64e6f64f8b285d7a02c8075d0a9fe4de43a57820391162b6" :answer/id #uuid "569f48cb-9521-5e81-be9c-fe6854117d7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection/0564c0b85cec1a90cb8274eaac90f139223a140fd87c89615cec97590bce7cc2" :answer/id #uuid "5fc72be4-3466-5430-9ce8-4474894c9ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection/963147949459cacb443b83f18608e8cf8017f38ac7ea2a5f5f253852bb91b096" :answer/id #uuid "fba5dd67-f5c0-5fd4-b041-70b89dd3594b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9598fdc9-a858-5d4b-a03d-901f79c31082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115985/selection/0564c0b85cec1a90cb8274eaac90f139223a140fd87c89615cec97590bce7cc2" :answer-field/choices ["q-115985/selection/144cf194032fb6a518bc4749829b072ca42940329a3c89593e06f6b9dbf7895a" "q-115985/selection/6cd7ba537c06f069990dfd5ed134e4f73bdea283b926cec942149fb50cdcfb3b" "q-115985/selection/b50005a2c8c4787e64e6f64f8b285d7a02c8075d0a9fe4de43a57820391162b6" "q-115985/selection/0564c0b85cec1a90cb8274eaac90f139223a140fd87c89615cec97590bce7cc2" "q-115985/selection/963147949459cacb443b83f18608e8cf8017f38ac7ea2a5f5f253852bb91b096"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115985" :question/id #uuid "36ab03d9-4172-552d-aa1a-9362a50cffe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115985" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115985/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115985"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-38509"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+2)(2x-3)}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection/11fe1e6d78f8b4a07521d7bf20bb5f51cc94e1f3b8d9d0667d88515b407448fe" :answer/id #uuid "ec0ea393-2389-5f01-afd2-2310c5a83a22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection/9db0efa0948cf6c66c19d7e27ce3575bd938c29862489347745fb8087459262f" :answer/id #uuid "597ecae0-59e3-5ee0-8fe7-9157957a394e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection/593f8a365dd97e97f40b868be86570d6ab012406388505c1f9c38a579cfe5893" :answer/id #uuid "012c8ee1-457b-5aca-85d9-2d0c1b4ecbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection/00b1266816fe404346e04ac6630be8d5f2a1a8a1665fd707bbf478e5fedc5beb" :answer/id #uuid "4650e979-d2d5-58d6-bacb-e0b3de8edc76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection/73f602772e504e63dc7a6c34338f268a2e0a6254e2226c028d58274eab484cd9" :answer/id #uuid "4f917a8b-27b9-5fec-8e97-ff3b79770e60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb62559-8655-5a3c-aef7-c52a7b19663a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38507/selection/11fe1e6d78f8b4a07521d7bf20bb5f51cc94e1f3b8d9d0667d88515b407448fe" :answer-field/choices ["q-38507/selection/11fe1e6d78f8b4a07521d7bf20bb5f51cc94e1f3b8d9d0667d88515b407448fe" "q-38507/selection/9db0efa0948cf6c66c19d7e27ce3575bd938c29862489347745fb8087459262f" "q-38507/selection/593f8a365dd97e97f40b868be86570d6ab012406388505c1f9c38a579cfe5893" "q-38507/selection/00b1266816fe404346e04ac6630be8d5f2a1a8a1665fd707bbf478e5fedc5beb" "q-38507/selection/73f602772e504e63dc7a6c34338f268a2e0a6254e2226c028d58274eab484cd9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38507" :question/id #uuid "fc751058-54b8-5d08-a921-6b30403f4883" :question/math-academy-id "q-38507" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x}{x-2}<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(3x+1)}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{3},0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{3},0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38507/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38507"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "856dee23-cfa4-50b1-91a1-abecc6369359" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "693e7893-3964-53e8-9688-e95c1294ec9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e63c4d2b-8bdb-5929-92fa-32139690aea9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274626/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-274626/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" "q-274626/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "86b97f72-a094-57f6-ba1e-bd4ece62dcdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "10848d24-2032-594a-9280-2b22ed45f596" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274626/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-274626/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274626" :question/id #uuid "a8dae39b-5dc3-5782-b404-cf81501c2a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-274626" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}}{3-2x}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+1)}{3-2x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2{x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}\\,).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-274626/field-1" "q-274626/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325754/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "86fba9fe-b851-53ff-bba5-901e30b09e60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325754/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a2e99e-04d1-5af4-9ac9-11511cedbb54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325754/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer-field/choices ["q-325754/field-1/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325754/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "78112087-3dee-5a7e-a2d2-329c9990c632" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325754/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9ba9e3-863e-54c1-94f5-15d108007a0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325754/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-325754/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325754" :question/id #uuid "3a638bce-dba7-54c4-9d86-d025e9cf926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325754" :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{{x}^{2}+7x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{-4}{x(x+7)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)=0$ has no solution. On the other hand, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=-7$ and $x=0.$ These asymptotes divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-7,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)>0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-7,0\\,).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325754/field-1" "q-325754/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bfeec4f-a652-5945-80c5-e6faa1f8e3af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325754"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5859"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{1-x}{x+2} < 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\lt 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$ is $x = 1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x = -2.$ The root and asymptote divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1-x$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-2)$ and $(1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (1,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7749"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\geq 0.$\n\nThe solution to the equation $f(x) = 0$ is $x=\\dfrac 12.$ Also, $f(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x = -2$ and $x=1.$ The root and asymptotes divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right), \\quad \\left(-2,\\dfrac 12\\right), \\quad \\left(\\dfrac 12,1\\right), \\quad \\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-2\\right)$ | $\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12,1\\right)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\geq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $(1,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the root of $f(x)$ is also a solution, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\geq 0$ is \n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-2,\\dfrac12\\right]\\cup (1,\\infty).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5861"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3} \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-3} .\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) =\t\\dfrac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x = \\pm 1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-1) \\quad(-1,1) \\quad (1,3) \\quad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-1\\right)$ | $\\left(-1,1\\right)$ | $\\left(1,3\\right)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,-1]\\cup [1,3)$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
